# btop's dedicated domain may read public process statistics and send signals.
# Kernel credential checks still restrict signaling to the user's processes;
# this receiver permission grants no capability or executable transition.
# The distribution's base.d hook applies it to future ordinary app profiles.
signal (receive) peer=desktop-btop,
ptrace (readby) peer=desktop-btop,
# aa-status reads confinement labels and executable links, never process memory.
ptrace (readby) peer=desktop-launcher//apparmor-status,
# Foot/Kitty now use their own general command domain. Terminal close, Ctrl-C,
# timeout and process-status tools retain their reciprocal permissions. Kernel
# UID/capability checks still apply; existing explicit deny rules still win.
signal (receive) peer=desktop-terminal,
signal (send) set=(chld) peer=desktop-terminal,
ptrace (readby) peer=desktop-terminal,
